<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================= -->
<!--  Chapter 10: Accounting for Variability                              -->
<!-- ================================================================= -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-accounting-variability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Accounting for Variability</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-ch10-variability">
    <p>
      We looked at observing and measuring variability in
      <xref ref="ch-variability"/>; for example, how spread out values are
      around a centre, and how to put a number on that spread.
      We saw that life expectancy across African countries had a standard
      deviation of around 9.6 years in 2007, that Rwanda's collapse in 1992
      produced a squared distance from the mean so large it dominated the
      variance for that year, and that the IQR is more robust to such extremes
      than the standard deviation.
      Those are all answers to the question: <em>how much</em> does this vary?
    </p>

    <p>
      This chapter looks at <em>why</em> it varies.
      Or more precisely, what portion of the variation can be accounted for, and
      what portion remains unaccounted for.
      This is the distinction between the three types of variability introduced
      in <xref ref="sec-accounting-journeys"/>: accounted-for, unaccounted-for,
      and natural.
      Measuring tells you the size of the spread, while accounting for it tells
      you where it comes from.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-accounting-journeys">
    <title>The Same Journey, Every Day Different</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In August 2026, on a Friday evening, I was leaving Nairobi's CBD to
        catch a late flight home to London.
        I had a flight at 11:40pm and ordered an Uber around 7:30pm.
        The first driver appeared, then cancelled while I was outside looking
        for them — I had lost wifi in the chaos of the streets and could not
        track them through the app.
        The same thing happened with a second driver.
        When I finally got wifi, the second driver informed me they had gone up
        the road, and agreed to pick me up from a hotel across the road, but had
        to turn around first because of the one-way street.
        I waited 30 minutes on that pavement, surrounded by cars, and could not
        find them anywhere.
        It was completely gridlocked, and he was still turning around.
      </p>

      <p>
        I eventually walked through the traffic hoping to find wifi and order a
        new driver somewhere away from the worst of the gridlock.
        A worker outside a café noticed me standing on the pavement, clearly
        lost and increasingly frantic, and insisted I come in.
        She sat me down, connected me to the wifi, and gave me a moment to
        think.
        Without that kindness I am not sure what I would have done.
      </p>

      <p>
        I found a driver and the app said the third taxi was ten minutes away.
        It took forty-five.
        The app had estimated ten minutes — and I had believed it, because I had
        no reason not to.
        I sat watching the little car barely move, block after block, as the
        minutes ticked by and my flight window got smaller.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ask someone from Nairobi what happened, and they will tell you
        immediately: it was Friday evening, in the CBD, at 7:30pm.
        That is a journey you plan hours around, or you do not make it.
        The traffic leaving the central business district on a Friday evening is
        not unpredictable.
        It is entirely predictable.
        It is just that I did not know to predict it.
      </p>

      <p>
        In the previous chapter, we measured variability — how spread out values
        are, and how to summarise that spread in a single number.
        But measuring variability and understanding it are two different things.
        My taxi journey was variable for reasons — some of which I should have
        known, some of which I could not have known, and some of which were
        simply random.
        This chapter is about separating those three things.
      </p>

      <p>
        Imagine you had no information at all about how long a boda-boda ride
        across Nairobi would take.
        No knowledge of rush hour, no sense of which roads are usually busy, no
        awareness that Fridays are worse than Tuesdays.
        Every day, you would treat each journey as if it were identical to every
        other — and you would be wrong, repeatedly, in ways that cost you time,
        money, and patience.
        You would schedule the same amount of time for a 7am ride and a 5pm
        ride.
        You would be surprised, every single Friday afternoon, by the traffic.
        You would have no way to plan.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now imagine you know one thing: rush hour is slower.
        Suddenly you can plan.
        You leave earlier on the days that matter, and schedule more time when
        needed.
        You may still be wrong sometimes — you cannot know about the accident on
        Ngong Road, or the blocked junction in town — but you are systematically
        less wrong than before.
        Knowing even one source of variation and accounting for it makes your
        predictions better.
        My mistake on that Friday evening was not unavoidable.
        It was a failure to account for something that was entirely knowable.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is what I mean by the three types of variability.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some variation is <term>accounted-for variability</term>: you know what
        causes it, you have measured it, and you can use it to make better
        predictions.
        Time of day is exactly this — once you know it is 5pm on a Friday in
        Nairobi's CBD, you already know something useful about the journey
        ahead.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some variation is <term>unaccounted-for variability</term>: other things
        affect the journey — accidents, roadworks, the school term starting —
        but you have not measured them and cannot include them in your
        predictions.
        They are real causes of variation, making your predictions less reliable
        even when you have accounted for everything else you can.
      </p>

      <p>
        And some variation is just <term>natural variability</term>: the
        boda-boda rider caught a green light, or took a slightly different line
        through the roundabout.
        No amount of information would have predicted it.
        It simply happened.
      </p>

      <p>
        The problem is not the natural variability — any honest prediction has
        to live with it.
        The problem is mistaking unaccounted-for variation for natural
        variation.
        If you assume that everything you cannot account for is just noise, you
        will underestimate how wrong your predictions can be, and you will miss
        the fact that some of that wrongness has a cause that could, in
        principle, be found and understood.
        A boda-boda rider who thinks the variation in their journey times is
        just random bad luck will never think to check whether there is a
        pattern — whether certain roads are systematically worse, whether the
        school term changes things, whether the weather is a factor.
        Those patterns are real.
        Mistaking them for noise means never looking for them.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-accounting-traffic">
      <title>Accounting for Traffic Variation in Nairobi</title>

      <p>
        The visualisations by TomTom displayed in Ch9 make the three types of
        variability visible in a way that numbers alone cannot.
      </p>

      <p>
        Recall <xref ref="fig-tomtom-heatmap-week"/> which shows travel time
        across every hour of every day of the week, averaged across 2025.
        Each cell represents one two-hour slot on one day of the week; darker
        red means longer journey times, lighter pink means shorter.
        The pattern is immediately visible: weekday mornings and evenings are
        dark, weekend days are lighter, and the middle of the night is the
        lightest of all.
        Time is <term>accounted-for variability</term> made visible: the
        variation that follows a predictable pattern, week after week, and that
        anyone who knows Nairobi already carries in their head.
        If I had looked at this chart before ordering my Uber on that Friday
        evening in August, I would have seen immediately that 7:30pm on a Friday
        is one of the darker cells on the whole grid.
      </p>

      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-tomtom-map-morning"/> and
        <xref ref="fig-tomtom-map-evening"/> show where in Nairobi that
        congestion falls during the morning and evening rush hours — not just
        when, but where.
        Yellow hexagons indicate moderate congestion; orange and red indicate
        heavier delays.
        The CBD and the main arterial roads radiating outward from it are
        visibly darker than the outer suburbs.
        During the morning rush, the congestion concentrates in the city centre
        as commuters flow in; during the evening rush, it spreads more broadly
        as people try to get home.
        This spatial pattern is also displaying
        <term>accounted-for variability</term> — predictable, measurable, and
        useful for planning.
      </p>

      <p>
        But even knowing the time of day and the location is not enough to
        predict any specific journey perfectly.
        <xref ref="fig-tomtom-live-congestion"/> illustrates why.
        This is a screenshot I have just taken showing live congestion in
        Nairobi right now <fn>25th August 2026, 18:48 EAT</fn>, against the
        usual congestion level for the same time slot.
        The orange line is what is happening right now, the dotted yellow line
        is what typically happens at this time.
        On Monday morning, live congestion spiked to nearly 90 percent, well
        above the usual level of around 65 percent at that hour.
        On Tuesday afternoon, at the time this image was captured, live
        congestion was running at over 100 percent, higher than the typical
        level, and still climbing.
        Something was making that particular day worse than a typical Tuesday.
        Perhaps an accident, or roadworks, or nothing identifiable at all.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is <term>unaccounted-for variability</term> in action.
        TomTom's usual congestion line represents what the accounted-for
        variability predicts, the pattern that repeats week after week.
        The gap between that line and the live reading is the part that the
        pattern cannot account for.
        Some of that gap has a cause, something happened on that road on that
        day that pushed congestion above its normal level.
        And some of it is just <term>natural variability</term>: small
        fluctuations that remain even after everything predictable has been
        accounted for.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-tomtom-live-congestion">
        <caption>
          Live versus usual congestion level in Nairobi across Monday and
          Tuesday, with live speed shown below.
          The orange line shows actual congestion; the dotted yellow line shows
          the typical level for that time slot.
          The gap between the two lines represents variation that the usual
          pattern does not predict — unaccounted-for and natural variability.
          Source: TomTom Traffic Index, captured August 2026.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch9_tomtom_live_congestion.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A line chart showing congestion level as a percentage on the
              y-axis and time across Monday and Tuesday on the x-axis.
              An orange solid line shows live congestion and a dotted yellow
              line shows the usual congestion level.
              Both lines show peaks around 8am and 5pm on each day.
              On Monday afternoon, the orange live line spikes to nearly 90
              percent, substantially above the yellow usual line of around 65
              percent.
              On Tuesday afternoon, the live line reaches over 100 percent and
              continues rising at the right edge of the chart, labelled NOW,
              again running above the usual level.
              Below the congestion chart, a bar chart shows live speed in km/h,
              with speeds dropping to around 10 km/h at peak congestion periods.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <term>Sources:</term> TomTom Traffic Index 2025.
        Nairobi city profile.
        Available at:
        <url href="https://www.tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi/" visual="tomtom.com/traffic-index/city/nairobi"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ch10-stack">
    <title>Practise: Accounting for Variability</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-ch10-ten-minutes-away">
      <title>Accounting for Variability: Journey Time</title>

      <stack source="stack/Statistics-and-Probability-Open-Question-Bank/top/STACK-for-Statistics-and-Probability/Ch10-Ten-Minutes-Away-accounting-for-journey-time.xml" label="stk-ch10-ten-minutes-away"/>
    </exercise>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-anova-r">
    <title>Accounting for Variability in R: ANOVA Tables</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="ch-variability"/> we looked at how spread out life
        expectancy is across countries and over time, and explored how to put a
        number on that spread.
        This section asks the next question: how much of that variability can we
        account for, and with what?
        The tool for answering this is the <term>ANOVA table</term>.
        This can split the total variability in an outcome into the part that a
        variable accounts for, and the part that remains.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="sec-ch9-variability-illustration">
        <title>Illustration of Variability</title>

        <p>
          Before measuring variability, it helps to see what the different types
          actually look like.
          We will use a simple example throughout: investigating the
          relationship between a person's height and weight.
          We will start with a dataset that has no variability at all, then
          gradually introduce different sources of variability.
          These are deliberately simplified examples to help us understand the
          concepts.
        </p>

        <p>
          <term>No variability</term> Imagine measuring the heights and weights
          of ten people.
          In our first example, everyone weighs exactly 65 kg, regardless of
          their height.
          Although the people have different heights, there is no variability in
          their weights.
          Every point sits on a horizontal line.
          There is nothing to explain about why some people weigh more than
          others, because nobody does.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-no-variability">
          <caption>
            No variability in weight: Everyone weighs exactly 65 kg, regardless
            of their height.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch9_no_variability.png" width="70%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A scatter plot of height against weight.
                All ten points sit at exactly 65 kg, forming a horizontal line.
                There is no variability in weight.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          <term>Accounted-for variability only</term> Now imagine that weight
          depends entirely on height.
          Taller people weigh more, and for every additional centimetre of
          height, weight increases by exactly 0.8 kg.
          There is now variability in weight: some people weigh more than
          others.
          But all of this variability can be accounted for by height.
          If we know someone's height, we can predict their weight exactly.
          There are no surprises and no additional variability around the
          relationship.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-accounted-for-only">
          <caption>
            Accounted-for variability only.
            Weight increases with height, and every point falls exactly on the
            same line.
            All variability in weight is accounted for by height.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch9_accounted for_only.png" width="70%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A scatter plot of height against weight.
                All ten points fall exactly on an upward-sloping straight line.
                There is variability in weight, but no scatter around the
                relationship with height.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          <term>Accounted-for and natural variability</term> Of course, in
          reality, two people of the same height will not necessarily weigh the
          same.
          Let's introduce some random variation into our example.
          Taller people still tend to weigh more, but their weights no longer
          follow the relationship perfectly.
          Some people weigh a little more than we would predict from their
          height, and others weigh a little less.
          Height still accounts for some of the variability in weight.
          However, there is now additional variability around the relationship,
          which we are representing here using random noise.
          This variation that remains even after we have accounted for height is
          the natural variability in our simplified example.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-accounted-for-natural">
          <caption>
            Accounted-for and natural variability.
            Height accounts for the overall upward trend, while random variation
            causes individual weights to fall above or below the expected
            relationship (shown by the dashed line).
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch9_accounted for_natural.png" width="70%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A scatter plot of height against weight.
                Ten points are scattered around an upward-sloping dashed line.
                The relationship between height and weight remains visible, but
                the points no longer fall exactly on the line.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          <term>Accounted-for, unaccounted-for, and natural variability</term>
          Finally, imagine that we have another variable influencing weight:
          sex.
          In this simplified example, we will assume that males weigh, on
          average, 10 kg more than females of the same height.
          This is an illustrative assumption, rather than a rule about
          individual people's weights!
          However, suppose our dataset only contains height and weight.
          We have not recorded sex.
          If we analyse weight using height alone, we can still see the general
          relationship: taller people tend to weigh more.
          But we cannot explain all the differences between people's weights.
          Some of those differences are systematic: they are associated with
          sex, a variable we have not included in our analysis.
          Other differences are the random variation we introduced earlier.
          This gives us our three sources of variability: variability accounted
          for by height, variability associated with sex that we have not
          accounted for, and natural variability around these relationships.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-all-variability">
          <caption>
            Accounted-for, unaccounted-for, and natural variability.
            Height explains some of the differences in weight, but the points
            also vary around the fitted line.
            Some of this remaining variability is associated with sex, which was
            not recorded in the dataset, while some comes from random variation.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch9_all_variability.png" width="70%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A scatter plot of height against weight with an upward-sloping
                fitted regression line.
                The points are more widely scattered around the line than in the
                previous example.
                The analyst cannot distinguish the effect of sex from random
                variation using the available variables alone.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          <term>What happens when we include sex?</term> What if we now discover
          that sex was recorded after all?
          We can plot exactly the same data again, but this time colour the
          points according to sex.
          We can also fit separate lines for males and females.
          Notice that the points now form two groups, with males tending to have
          higher weights than females of the same height.
          The separate lines help us see a relationship that was hidden in the
          previous graph.
          Some of the variability that was previously unaccounted for can now be
          accounted for by sex.
          However, the points still do not fall perfectly on their respective
          lines.
          There is still some variability remaining.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch9-sex-revealed">
          <caption>
            The same height and weight data as in
            <xref ref="fig-ch9-all-variability"/>, now coloured by sex.
            Separate fitted lines reveal that males tend to weigh more than
            females of the same height in this simulated dataset.
            Accounting for sex explains some of the variability that was
            previously unaccounted for.
          </caption>

          <image source="images/ch9_sex_revealed.png" width="70%">
            <description>
              <p>
                A scatter plot of height against weight, using the same ten
                observations as the previous graph.
                Points are coloured by sex, with separate upward-sloping fitted
                lines for males and females.
                The male line is generally above the female line.
                Some scatter remains around both lines.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          This final example illustrates an important distinction.
          Just because we cannot explain some variability does not mean it is
          random.
          When we only had height and weight, the differences around the fitted
          line appeared unexplained.
          But once we included sex, we could account for some of those
          differences.
          In real datasets, we rarely have every variable that might influence
          an outcome.
          Other factors, such as age, diet, or physical activity, might also
          help explain differences in weight.
          The aim is not necessarily to explain every difference between
          observations, but to understand how much variability we can account
          for with the information available, and recognise what remains
          unexplained.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        We use the same Gapminder data from <xref ref="sec-gapminder"/>.
        Load it if you have not already:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
          # load in the data
        library(gapminder)
        library(ggplot2)
        library(dplyr)

        data(gapminder)
        head(gapminder)
        </code>
      </program>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-anova-gdp">
      <title>A numerical variable: GDP per capita</title>

      <p>
        We start with a question: how much of the variation in life expectancy
        across countries and years can be accounted for by GDP per capita?
        Wealthier countries tend to have longer life expectancies, but how much
        of the total spread does that relationship actually account for?
      </p>

      <p>
        Recall from <xref ref="sec-gapminder"/>, shown in
        <xref ref="fig-ch10-gdp-raw"/> that the relationship between GDP and
        life expectancy is not linear, it curves, and a log scale makes it much
        cleaner.
        Let us apply that transformation before continuing:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder, aes(x = gdpPercap, y = lifeExp)) +
          geom_point(colour = "#636363") +
          labs(x = "GDP per capita", y = "Life expectancy (years)") +
          theme_bw()
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch10-gdp-raw">
        <caption>
          Life expectancy against GDP per capita, untransformed.
          The relationship is clearly curved, with a cluster of countries
          compressed at the low end of the x-axis.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch10_gdp_raw.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A scatter plot with GDP per capita on the x-axis and life
              expectancy in years on the y-axis.
              Most points are clustered toward the left of the x-axis, with a
              long tail of wealthier countries stretching to the right.
              The relationship is strongly curved rather than linear.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        After log-transforming <c>gdpPercap</c>, we fit an ANOVA.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        gapminder &lt;- gapminder %&gt;%
          mutate(log_gdpPercap = log(gdpPercap))

        mod &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ log_gdpPercap, data = gapminder)
        anova(mod)
        #&gt; Analysis of Variance Table
        #&gt;
        #&gt; Response: lifeExp
        #&gt;               Df  Sum Sq Mean Sq F value    Pr(&gt;F)
        #&gt; log_gdpPercap  1  185335  185335  3192.3 &lt;2.2e-16 ***
        #&gt; Residuals   1702   98814      58
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        The ANOVA table partitions the total variability in life expectancy into
        two rows.
        The first row is the variability that GDP per capita accounts for:
        185,335 units of squared variation.
        The second row is the variability left over after accounting for GDP:
        98,814 units.
        The total is 185,335 + 98,814 = 284,149.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        185335 / 284149
        #&gt; [1] 0.652
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        That's 65.2%!
        65.2% of the variability in life expectancy is accounted for by log GDP
        per capita.
        That is a striking number: Simply knowing how wealthy a country is, on a
        log scale, accounts for nearly two thirds of the variation in how long
        its people live, across all countries and all years in the dataset.
      </p>

      <p>
        The three plots below make this partition visible.
        The first shows the <term>total sum of squares</term>: the orange
        segments are the distances from each country's life expectancy to the
        overall mean (the horizontal line).
        This is all the variability we are trying to account for <ndash/> and
        sums to that 284,149 in the ANOVA table.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch10-total-ss">
        <caption>
          Total sum of squares.
          Each orange segment shows the distance from one country-year's life
          expectancy to the overall mean.
          The sum of all squared segment lengths is 284,149.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch10_total_ss.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A scatter plot with log GDP per capita on the x-axis and life
              expectancy on the y-axis.
              A horizontal black line runs across at the mean life expectancy.
              Orange vertical segments connect each point to that line.
              Segments are longer for points far from the mean — very low and
              very high life expectancies.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The second plot shows the <term>accounted-for sum of squares</term>: the
        distances from the fitted line to the overall mean, measured at each
        data point.
        This is the variability that log GDP per capita accounts for.
        The reduction in spread achieved by using the line rather than the mean.
        This sums to that 185,335 value.
      </p>

      <insight>
        <title>Linking to Chapter 9</title>

        <p>
          The reduction in square area from left to right in
          <xref ref="fig-ch9-least-squares-comparison"/>, from the flat line to
          the line of best fit — is exactly what the ANOVA table's accounted for
          sum of squares measures.
          The flat line at the mean is the world with nothing accounted for:
          total SS only.
          The line of best fit reduces that area by the accounted for SS,
          leaving the residual SS in the smaller squares that remain.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch10-accounted-for-ss">
        <caption>
          Accounted-for sum of squares.
          Each orange segment shows the distance from one country-year's life
          expectancy to the fitted line.
          The sum of squared segment lengths is 185,335 — the variability that
          log GDP per capita accounts for.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch10_explained_ss.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              The same scatter plot with a fitted regression line.
              Orange segments now connect each point to the line of best fit
              rather than the horizontal mean.
              The segments are shorter overall than in the total SS plot,
              reflecting that the line fits better than the mean.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The third plot shows the <term>residual sum of squares</term>: the
        leftover (residual) sums of squares.
        This gives the distances for each point from the line of best fit to the
        overall mean.
        These are the residuals — the variability that log GDP per capita does
        not account for.
        This sum equals that 98,814 value from the ANOVA table.
      </p>

      <insight>
        <title>Linking to Chapter 9</title>

        <p>
          The squares shrinking as the line rotates in
          <xref ref="fig-ch9-least-squares-animation"/> from horizontal to its
          best position are the residuals — the variability the line cannot
          account for.
          A small total squared error at the end of the animation means the line
          accounts for most of the variability; a large one means much of the
          variability is still unaccounted for.
          The ANOVA table puts a number on exactly that.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        ggplot(gapminder, aes(x = log_gdpPercap, y = lifeExp)) +
          geom_segment(
            aes(xend = log_gdpPercap,
                yend = predict(lm(lifeExp ~ log_gdpPercap, 
                                  data = gapminder))),
            colour = "orange", alpha = 0.5) +
          geom_point(colour = "#636363") +
          geom_smooth(se = FALSE, method = "lm", colour = "black") +
          labs(title = "Residual sum of squares",
               subtitle = "Orange: distance from each point to the line of best fit",
               x = "Log GDP per capita",
               y = "Life expectancy (years)") +
          theme_bw()
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch10-residual-ss">
        <caption>
          Residual sum of squares.
          Each orange segment shows the distance from the fitted line to the
          overall mean, at each data point's x value.
          The sum of all squared segment lengths is 98,814: the variability that
          log GDP per capita cannot account for.
          Total SS = accounted for SS + Residual SS: 284,149 = 185,335 + 98,814.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch10_residual_ss.png" width="80%">
          <description>
            <p>
              The same scatter plot with both the fitted line and the horizontal
              mean line.
              Orange segments connect the fitted line to the mean line at each x
              value.
              Where the fitted line is well above or below the mean, the
              segments are long.
              Where the fitted line crosses or is close to the mean, the
              segments are short.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The three sum of squares are related by a simple identity: Total SS =
        Accounted for SS + Residual SS (284,149 = 185,335 + 98,814).
        The ANOVA table reports exactly these three values, and the fraction
        accounted for by the variable is 65.2%.
      </p>

      <insight>
        <title>What if we did not log-transform? (Advanced)</title>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        mod &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ gdpPercap, data = gapminder)
        anova(mod)
        #&gt; Analysis of Variance Table
        #&gt;
        #&gt; Response: lifeExp
        #&gt;            Df  Sum Sq Mean Sq F value    Pr(&gt;F)
        #&gt; gdpPercap   1   96813   96813  879.58 &lt;2.2e-16 ***
        #&gt; Residuals 1702  187335     110
        #&gt; Total     1703  284148     167
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          The ANOVA table partitions the total variability in life expectancy
          into two rows.
          The first row is the variability that GDP per capita accounts for:
          96,813 units of squared variation.
          The second row is the variability left over after accounting for GDP:
          187,335 units.
          The total is 96,813 + 187,335 = 284,148.
        </p>

        <p>
          The fraction accounted for is:
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        96813 / (96813 + 187335)
        #&gt; [1] 0.341
          </code>
        </program>

        <p>
          GDP per capita accounts for 34.1% of the variability in life
          expectancy.
          That is already substantial: knowing a country's wealth tells you
          something real about how long its people live.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-anova-continent">
      <title>A categorical variable: continent</title>

      <p>
        The same framework applies when the explanatory variable is categorical
        rather than numerical.
        Instead of a line of best fit, we now fit a separate mean for each
        continent.
        How much of the variability in life expectancy is accounted for by
        simply knowing which continent a country is on?
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        mod_continent &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ continent, data = gapminder)
        anova(mod_continent)
        #&gt; Analysis of Variance Table
        #&gt;
        #&gt; Response: lifeExp
        #&gt;            Df  Sum Sq Mean Sq F value    Pr(&gt;F)
        #&gt; continent   4  139343   34836  408.73 &lt;2.2e-16 ***
        #&gt; Residuals 1699  144805      85
        #&gt; Total     1703  284149

        139343 / 284149
        #&gt; [1] 0.490
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Continent alone accounts for 49.0% of the variability in life
        expectancy.
        The degrees of freedom for continent is 4 because there are five
        continents, and fitting a separate mean for each uses four degrees of
        freedom (the fifth is determined once the other four are fixed).
      </p>

      <p>
        The plots below show the same three components as before, now for the
        continent model.
        Instead of a single line of best fit, the horizontal segments in each
        plot represent the continent mean: a separate value for each group.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch10-continent-accounted-for">
        <caption>
          Accounted for sum of squares for the continent model.
          Each orange segment shows the distance from a country-year's continent
          mean (black horizontal line) to their life expectancy point.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch10_continent_accountedfor.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A jittered dot plot with one column per continent.
              Short horizontal segments mark each continent's mean life
              expectancy.
              Orange vertical segments connect the continent mean to the life
              expectancy for each point.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch10-continent-total">
        <caption>
          Total sum of squares for the continent model.
          Each orange segment shows the distance from a country-year's life
          expectancy to the overall mean (horizontal line).
          Africa tends to have its mean life expectancy far below the global
          mean, and Oceania far above.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch10_continent_total.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A jittered dot plot with one column per continent.
              A single horizontal line marks the overall mean life expectancy.
              Orange vertical segments connect each life expectancy point to the
              overall mean for each point.
              Africa's column generally sits well below the overall mean;
              Oceania sits well above it.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch10-continent-unaccounted-for">
        <caption>
          Unaccounted for sum of squares for the continent model.
          Each orange segment shows the distance from a country-year's continent
          mean (short horizontal line) to the overall mean (long horizontal
          line).
          Continents whose mean life expectancy is far from the global mean —
          Africa below, Oceania above — contribute the most to the SS.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch10_continent_unaccountedfor.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              A jittered dot plot with one column per continent.
              Short horizontal segments mark each continent's mean life
              expectancy.
              A long horizontal line marks the overall mean.
              Orange vertical segments connect the continent mean to the overall
              mean for each point.
              Africa's column sits well below the overall mean; Oceania and
              Europe sit well above it.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-anova-combined">
      <title>Combining variables</title>

      <p>
        Both log GDP per capita and continent account for substantial fractions
        of the variability in life expectancy.
        But how much do they account for together?
        We can add both to the same model:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        mod_combined &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ continent + log_gdpPercap, 
                           data = gapminder)
        anova(mod_combined)
        #&gt; Analysis of Variance Table
        #&gt;
        #&gt; Response: lifeExp
        #&gt;               Df  Sum Sq Mean Sq  F value    Pr(&gt;F)
        #&gt; continent      4  139343   34836   703.24 &lt;2.2e-16 ***
        #&gt; log_gdpPercap  1   60693   60693  1225.22 &lt;2.2e-16 ***
        #&gt; Residuals   1698   84113      50

        (139343 + 60693) / 284149
        #&gt; [1] 0.704
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Together, continent and log GDP per capita account for 70.4% of the
        variability in life expectancy.
        Notice that log GDP per capita's sum of squares has dropped from 185,335
        (when it was the only variable) to 60,693 (when continent is already in
        the model).
        This is because continent and GDP are related — richer continents tend
        to have higher GDP — so some of the variability that log GDP appeared to
        account for on its own was actually shared with continent.
        Once continent is accounted for first, log GDP accounts for less
        additional variability.
        The same would happen if we entered log GDP first and then continent.
        However, regardless of order, the two variables together account for the
        same total variability.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-anova-more">
      <title>Adding more variables</title>

      <p>
        How far can we push this?
        Let us add country, year, and population to the model.
        Country is more specific than continent — knowing which country a row
        belongs to tells you more than knowing which continent — so it replaces
        continent in the model.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        mod_full &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ log_gdpPercap + country + year + pop,
                       data = gapminder)
        anova(mod_full)
        #&gt; Analysis of Variance Table
        #&gt;
        #&gt; Response: lifeExp
        #&gt;               Df  Sum Sq Mean Sq   F value    Pr(&gt;F)
        #&gt; log_gdpPercap  1  185335  185335 15165.874 &lt;2.2e-16 ***
        #&gt; country       141   55179     391    32.023 &lt;2.2e-16 ***
        #&gt; year           1   24205   24205  1980.712 &lt;2.2e-16 ***
        #&gt; pop            1     378     378    30.923 3.154e-08 ***
        #&gt; Residuals   1559   19052      12

        (185335 + 55179 + 24205 + 378) / 284149
        #&gt; [1] 0.933
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Log GDP per capita, country, year, and population together account for
        93.3% of the variability in life expectancy.
        The remaining 6.7% (19,052 units of variability) sits in the residuals.
        Some of that is unaccounted-for variability with real causes: specific
        events in specific countries in specific years that none of these four
        variables can capture.
        Some is natural variability — the irreducible noise that would remain
        even if we had measured everything measurable.
        The ANOVA table cannot separate these two.
        That distinction requires domain knowledge; for example, knowing that
        Rwanda in 1992 is explained by the troubles in Rwanda.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-anova-residual">
        <title>The residual is not just noise</title>

        <p>
          The residual sum of squares is what remains after the named variables
          have accounted for what they can.
          It is tempting to call this noise and move on, but the residual
          contains two very different things: genuine random variation that no
          variable would ever account for, and real variation caused by things
          that were not measured or not included.
          Rwanda's life expectancy in 1992 sits in the residual of any model
          that does not include a variable for genocide.
          The ANOVA table reports its squared distance from the fitted value as
          part of the 19,052.
          Only context tells you that it is not noise.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        The ANOVA table shows that population accounts for 378 units of
        variability in life expectancy.
        But does a country having a larger population cause its people to live
        longer?
        Almost certainly not.
        Population is associated with life expectancy in this dataset — perhaps
        because larger populations tend to be in countries that have urbanised
        and developed more — but that association is not a causal mechanism.
        The same is true of year: life expectancy in Kenya changes as year
        increases, and year accounts for a large fraction of that variability.
        But the year ticking over from 1987 to 1992 did not cause life
        expectancy to fall.
        Other factors, such as the HIV/AIDS epidemic, did.
        Year is a proxy for all the things that changed over time (medicine,
        sanitation, nutrition, disease) none of which are in this dataset.
      </p>

      <p>
        This is the distinction between <term>accounting for</term> variability
        and <term>explaining</term> it.
        A variable accounts for variability when knowing its value lets you
        predict the outcome better — when it reduces the residual sum of
        squares.
        It explains variability when it is part of the actual causal mechanism
        driving the outcome.
        Year and population account for variability in life expectancy.
        They do not explain it.
        The ANOVA table cannot tell you which is which.
        That requires knowing something about the world beyond the data.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-anova-table-reading">
      <title>Reading the ANOVA table</title>

      <p>
        The ANOVA table has five columns.
        The first three — <c>Df</c>, <c>Sum Sq</c>, and <c>Mean Sq</c> —
        describe the partition of variability.
        Then <c>F value</c> helps show how much larger the variability accounted
        for by the variable is compared to the residual.
        <c>Pr(&gt;F)</c> can be completely ignored in this book.
        For the purposes of this chapter, focus on the first three.
      </p>

      <tabular halign="left">
        <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
        <col halign="left" width="80%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
          <cell>Column</cell>
          <cell>What it means</cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>Df</c></cell>
          <cell>
            Degrees of freedom.
            For a numerical variable, always 1.
            For a categorical variable with <m>k</m> levels, always
            <m>k - 1</m>.
            For residuals, total rows minus the number of parameters fitted.
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>Sum Sq</c></cell>
          <cell>
            Sum of squares — the total squared variation accounted for by this
            variable, or left in the residual.
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>Mean Sq</c></cell>
          <cell>
            Mean square — Sum Sq divided by Df.
            The average squared variation per degree of freedom.
          </cell>
        </row>

        <row>
          <cell><c>F value</c></cell>
          <cell>
            The ratio of the variable's Mean Sq to the residual Mean Sq.
            Large values indicate the variable accounts for substantially more
            variability than the residual.
          </cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>

      <insight xml:id="insight-boxplot-variability-types">
        <title>Two kinds of variability in one chart</title>

        <p>
          The chart in <xref ref="fig-ch9-africa-boxplot-years"/> shows two
          distinct kinds of variability at the same time, and it is worth
          separating them.
        </p>

        <p>
          The first is
          <term>variability across countries within a single 
  year</term>,
          shown by the height of each box.
          In any given year, some African countries have life expectancies well
          above the median and some well below.
          What drives this?
          Some of it could in principle be accounted for — if we had data on
          each country's wealth, access to healthcare, sanitation
          infrastructure, or disease burden, we could account for some of why
          one country sits above the box and another sits below it.
          The gapminder dataset gives us GDP per capita as a rough proxy for
          some of those things, but not all.
          Some drivers are harder to capture in data at all, and would hence be
          unaccounted for; e.g., governance quality, geography, the strength of
          public health systems, the legacy of particular conflicts.
          And some of the remaining variation between countries is simply
          natural: even two countries that look identical on every variable we
          can measure will not have exactly the same life expectancy.
        </p>

        <p>
          The second is <term>variability across years within Kenya</term>,
          illustrated by the rise and fall of the teal line.
          Kenya's life expectancy in 1952 was around 42 years; by 1987 it had
          risen to nearly 60; by 2002 it had fallen back to around 51, before
          recovering again.
          Again, some of it could in principle be accounted for.
          If we had data on improvements in medicine, sanitation, and nutrition
          over time, or on the spread of the HIV/AIDS epidemic, we could account
          for much of the rise and the subsequent dip.
          But we do not have those variables here.
          Some drivers are harder to capture in data at all; e.g., droughts,
          political instability, or specific health policy decisions in
          particular years.
          These all affected Kenyan life expectancy but are rarely available as
          measured variables.
          And some is natural: even in a stable period, life expectancy
          fluctuates slightly from year to year for reasons no dataset would
          ever fully capture.
        </p>
      </insight>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-ch10">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-variability-types">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each of the following situations, identify which type of
          variability is being described (accounted-for, unaccounted-for, or
          natural) and briefly explain your reasoning.
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Matatu journey times in Nairobi are consistently longer on Friday
              evenings than on Tuesday mornings.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Life expectancy in several southern African countries dropped
              sharply in the 1990s due to the HIV/AIDS epidemic, but this
              variable was not included in the dataset being analysed.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              A farmer's crop yield varies from year to year.
              The analyst has data on rainfall but not on soil quality, which
              also affects yield.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              A model predicting crop yield from rainfall accounts for 65% of
              the variation in yield across farms.
              The remaining 35% includes differences in soil quality that were
              not measured.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              After accounting for soil quality and rainfall, a small amount of
              variation in crop yield remains that cannot be attributed to any
              identifiable factor.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Accounted-for.</term> Day of the week and time of day are
              things we know and can measure, and the pattern repeats week after
              week.
              This is exactly the Friday evening traffic from the start of the
              chapter: once you know it's Friday evening, you can plan for it.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Unaccounted-for.</term> The HIV/AIDS epidemic is a very real
              cause of the drop in life expectancy, so this definitely isn't
              natural variability.
              But because it isn't in the dataset, any analysis of that data
              can't account for it.
              It ends up in the residual, looking like noise when it really
              isn't.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Unaccounted-for</term> (for soil quality).
              Rainfall is measured, so the part of the yield that rainfall
              accounts for is accounted-for variability.
              Soil quality also affects yield, but it hasn't been measured, so
              the variation it causes is unaccounted-for.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Accounted-for</term> (the 65%), with the remaining 35% a
              mix.
              Rainfall accounts for 65% of the variation in yield.
              The other 35% isn't all natural, though.
              Part of it comes from differences in soil quality, which is a
              real, identifiable cause we just didn't measure, so that part is
              unaccounted-for.
              Whatever is left after that would be natural variability.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Natural.</term> We've accounted for both rainfall and soil
              quality, and what remains can't be attributed to anything we can
              identify.
              Of course, we can never be completely sure that there isn't
              another factor we haven't thought of!
              If someone later found one (say, the timing of planting), part of
              this <q>natural</q> variation would turn out to have been
              unaccounted-for all along.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-accounting-rsquared">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using the <c>gapminder</c> dataset filtered to 2007:
        </p>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Fit an ANOVA predicting life expectancy from GDP per capita for
              African countries only.
              What is the R-squared?
              How much variability does GDP account for?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Now fit the same ANOVA for Asian countries only.
              Is the R-squared higher or lower than for Africa?
              What might account for the difference?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Which five countries have the largest positive residuals in the
              African ANOVA?
              Which five have the largest negative residuals?
              What do the negative residuals have in common?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Produce the ANOVA table for all countries.
              How much of the total variability does continent account for?
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              To the ANOVA for all countries, add GDP per capita to it alongside
              continent.
              Does adding GDP account for additional variability beyond what
              continent already accounts for?
              How much?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          First, we filter to 2007 and log-transform GDP per capita, just as we
          did in <xref ref="ss-anova-gdp"/>.
        </p>

        <program language="r">
          <code>
        gapminder_2007 &lt;- gapminder %&gt;%
          filter(year == 2007) %&gt;%
          mutate(log_gdpPercap = log(gdpPercap))

        africa_2007 &lt;- gapminder_2007 %&gt;% filter(continent == "Africa")
        asia_2007 &lt;- gapminder_2007 %&gt;% filter(continent == "Asia")
          </code>
        </program>

        <ol>
          <li>
            <program language="r">
              <code>
        mod_africa &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ log_gdpPercap, data = africa_2007)
        anova(mod_africa)
        #&gt; Analysis of Variance Table
        #&gt;
        #&gt; Response: lifeExp
        #&gt;               Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value    Pr(&gt;F)
        #&gt; log_gdpPercap  1  964.5  964.49  12.806 0.0007793 ***
        #&gt; Residuals     50 3765.9   75.32

        964.5 / (964.5 + 3765.9)
        #&gt; [1] 0.204
              </code>
            </program>

            <p>
              The R-squared is 0.204, so log GDP per capita accounts for only
              about 20% of the variability in life expectancy across African
              countries in 2007.
              That's much lower than the 65.2% we found for all countries and
              all years.
              Within Africa, knowing how wealthy a country is tells us
              surprisingly little about how long its people live.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <program language="r">
              <code>
        mod_asia &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ log_gdpPercap, data = asia_2007)
        anova(mod_asia)
        #&gt; Analysis of Variance Table
        #&gt;
        #&gt; Response: lifeExp
        #&gt;               Df  Sum Sq Mean Sq F value    Pr(&gt;F)
        #&gt; log_gdpPercap  1 1299.99 1299.99  55.244 2.263e-08 ***
        #&gt; Residuals     31  729.48   23.53

        1299.99 / (1299.99 + 729.48)
        #&gt; [1] 0.641
              </code>
            </program>

            <p>
              For Asia the R-squared is much higher, at 0.641.
              GDP accounts for around 64% of the variability, more than three
              times as much as in Africa.
            </p>

            <p>
              Why the difference?
              In Africa, something other than wealth is pulling some countries a
              long way from where their GDP would put them.
              The biggest candidate in 2007 is the HIV/AIDS epidemic, which hit
              some of the continent's wealthier countries, such as Botswana and
              South Africa, particularly hard.
              Conflict is another.
              Neither of these is in our model, so they sit in the residual as
              unaccounted-for variability.
              We'll see this more clearly in the next part.
              (Asia also has fewer countries in the data, 33 compared with 52,
              so we should be a little careful comparing the two.)
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              We can add the residuals to the data and sort by them.
            </p>

            <program language="r">
              <code>
        africa_2007 %&gt;%
          mutate(residual = resid(mod_africa)) %&gt;%
          arrange(desc(residual)) %&gt;%
          select(country, lifeExp, gdpPercap, residual) %&gt;%
          head(5)
        #&gt;   country lifeExp gdpPercap residual
        #&gt; 1 Reunion  76.442 7670.1226 15.42127
        #&gt; 2 Tunisia  73.923 7092.9230 13.23562
        #&gt; 3 Morocco  71.164 3820.1752 13.11325
        #&gt; 4 Comoros  65.152  986.1479 12.87152
        #&gt; 5 Algeria  72.301 6223.3675 12.17088

        africa_2007 %&gt;%
          mutate(residual = resid(mod_africa)) %&gt;%
          arrange(residual) %&gt;%
          select(country, lifeExp, gdpPercap, residual) %&gt;%
          head(5)
        #&gt;        country lifeExp gdpPercap  residual
        #&gt; 1    Swaziland  39.613  4513.481 -19.14834
        #&gt; 2       Angola  42.731  4797.231 -16.29013
        #&gt; 3 South Africa  49.339  9269.658 -12.48880
        #&gt; 4     Botswana  50.728 12569.852 -12.39747
        #&gt; 5      Lesotho  42.592  1569.331 -11.66807
              </code>
            </program>

            <p>
              The largest positive residuals are Reunion, Tunisia, Morocco,
              Comoros and Algeria.
              People there live longer than their GDP alone would predict.
              Three of these are in North Africa, and Reunion is an overseas
              department of France.
            </p>

            <p>
              The largest negative residuals are Swaziland, Angola, South
              Africa, Botswana and Lesotho.
              What do they have in common?
              They are all in southern Africa.
              Swaziland, South Africa, Botswana and Lesotho had some of the
              highest HIV prevalence rates in the world in 2007, and Botswana
              and South Africa are among the wealthiest countries on the
              continent.
              So their life expectancy is far below what their GDP would
              suggest.
              Angola is a bit different: its civil war only ended in 2002.
            </p>

            <p>
              This is the residual not being just noise, exactly as we saw in
              <xref ref="insight-anova-residual"/>.
              These five countries aren't randomly unlucky.
              There are real causes behind their residuals that GDP can't
              capture.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <program language="r">
              <code>
        mod_continent_2007 &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ continent, data = gapminder_2007)
        anova(mod_continent_2007)
        #&gt; Analysis of Variance Table
        #&gt;
        #&gt; Response: lifeExp
        #&gt;            Df  Sum Sq Mean Sq F value    Pr(&gt;F)
        #&gt; continent   4 13060.7  3265.2  59.714 &lt; 2.2e-16 ***
        #&gt; Residuals 137  7491.2    54.7

        13060.7 / (13060.7 + 7491.2)
        #&gt; [1] 0.635
              </code>
            </program>

            <p>
              Continent accounts for 63.5% of the variability in life expectancy
              across all countries in 2007.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <program language="r">
              <code>
        mod_both_2007 &lt;- lm(lifeExp ~ continent + log_gdpPercap,
                            data = gapminder_2007)
        anova(mod_both_2007)
        #&gt; Analysis of Variance Table
        #&gt;
        #&gt; Response: lifeExp
        #&gt;                Df  Sum Sq Mean Sq F value    Pr(&gt;F)
        #&gt; continent       4 13060.7  3265.2  92.880 &lt; 2.2e-16 ***
        #&gt; log_gdpPercap   1  2710.1  2710.1  77.092 6.136e-15 ***
        #&gt; Residuals     136  4781.0    35.2

        2710.1 / (13060.7 + 2710.1 + 4781.0)
        #&gt; [1] 0.132

        (13060.7 + 2710.1) / (13060.7 + 2710.1 + 4781.0)
        #&gt; [1] 0.767
              </code>
            </program>

            <p>
              Yes, it does.
              Once continent is in the model, log GDP per capita accounts for
              another 2,710 units of variability, which is 13.2% of the total.
              Together, continent and GDP account for 76.7%.
            </p>

            <p>
              So GDP still tells us something beyond which continent a country
              is on.
              But notice the order matters for how we read the table.
              Because continent goes in first, the variability that continent
              and GDP share is credited to continent.
              The 13.2% is only the <em>extra</em> that GDP accounts for on top
              of that, just as we saw in <xref ref="ss-anova-combined"/>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </solution>
    </exercise>

